UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 20, Lesson 4: Unit review and check-in', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Money unit review connects totalling, comparing, change, rounding and budget decisions.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same method to a larger number or a new context, then compare two solution paths for accuracy.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nMini review: total; change from $10 for $7.35; round $7.35; enough for another $2.50 item from leftover?\n\nFEEDBACK\nChange $2.65; rounds to $7; leftover after $7.35 from $10 is $2.65 so $2.50 fits.\n\nWATCH FOR\nReview that only lists dollars without cents hides place errors.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nComplete a 12-item money unit review. Show working for every item. List two skills to practise next.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Mathematics","learning_intention":"Money unit review connects totalling, comparing, change, rounding and budget decisions.","lesson_number":4,"lesson_role":"Fluency and review","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: place-value chart or number line"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["MA2-RN-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Complete a 12-item money unit review. Show working for every item. List two skills to practise next. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Change $2.65; rounds to $7; leftover after $7.35 from $10 is $2.65 so $2.50 fits. Reasoning: I used review by attending to change and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Review that only lists dollars without cents hides place errors. Correction: Change $2.65; rounds to $7; leftover after $7.35 from $10 is $2.65 so $2.50 fits. Why: the correction restores accurate use of review.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Review that only lists dollars without cents hides place errors. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Solve one fresh problem that uses review with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Describe one likely mistake with review, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require new numbers, visible working and named places/values. / Require a real misconception, corrected working and a method reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Change $2.65; rounds to $7; leftover after $7.35 from $10 is $2.65 so $2.50 fits. This matches the lesson skill for review.","options":["Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","Change $2.65; rounds to $7; leftover after $7.35 from $10 is $2.65 so $2.50 fits.","Review that only lists dollars without cents hides place errors.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer."],"question":"Mini review: total; change from $10 for $7.35; round $7.35; enough for another $2.50 item from leftover?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Review that only lists dollars without cents hides place errors. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Money unit review connects totalling, comparing, change, rounding and budget decisions.","Change $2.65; rounds to $7; leftover after $7.35 from $10 is $2.65 so $2.50 fits.","Careful use of change and explain why it matters with clear evidence.","Review that only lists dollars without cents hides place errors."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising review?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named change and explain why it matters, not by copying the model.","options":["Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a missing detail as a proven fact.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the change and explain why it matters used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence."],"question":"A learner is practising review. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Mathematics answers need labelled working tied to place value or quantity.","options":["Show the places, values or steps and say why each step is valid.","Write only a final number with no working.","Change digits to look neater without checking place value.","Review that only lists dollars without cents hides place errors."],"question":"Which working note best supports a mathematics answer about review?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the method; the others break it.","options":["Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","Change $2.65; rounds to $7; leftover after $7.35 from $10 is $2.65 so $2.50 fits.","Review that only lists dollars without cents hides place errors."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Money unit review connects totalling, comparing, change, rounding and budget decisions. — which statement is true?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Disagreement is resolved by checking places, operations and the question focus.","options":["Recheck places, operations and whether the question asked for the quantity you computed.","Pick the longer-looking working automatically.","Delete units so the answers appear to match.","Average the two answers without checking either."],"question":"Which error should you repair first if two methods disagree?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Transfer requires new numbers with the same method.","options":["Solve a fresh problem with different numbers and explain the method.","Retype the worked model numbers as the answer.","Change only handwriting and keep the same numbers.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work."],"question":"Which practice choice shows transfer of review?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Useful feedback names the failed step and shows corrected working.","options":["Tell the learner to skip working and only keep the final digits.","Just add more digits until it looks longer.","Name the exact place or step that failed, then show corrected working for review.","Praise neat handwriting even when the places are wrong."],"question":"Which feedback comment is most useful after a mistake?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require new numbers, visible working and named places/values.","question":"Solve one fresh problem that uses review with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Change $2.65; rounds to $7; leftover after $7.35 from $10 is $2.65 so $2.50 fits. Reasoning: I used review by attending to change and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require a real misconception, corrected working and a method reason.","question":"Describe one likely mistake with review, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Common error: Review that only lists dollars without cents hides place errors. Correction: Change $2.65; rounds to $7; leftover after $7.35 from $10 is $2.65 so $2.50 fits. Why: the correction restores accurate use of review.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent working and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-mat-y3-w20-l4","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA mathematics alignment using verified outcome code wording where recorded in lesson-pipeline/sources; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise review.","Read the mission before you start calculating.","Keep the place or unit you are working with in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your working with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the place or distance that carries the method.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore place value or working.","Prefer accurate method over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your working and reason are both present.","Proofread digits, places and operation signs.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact place, value or distance before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key numbers.","Say what each number represents in the method.","A final answer alone is weaker than labelled working."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with stronger working.","Change the value or method, not only how neat it looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose fresh numbers that still use the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original working and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new numbers."]},"subject":"Number and Algebra","success_criteria":["I can use review with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify change and explain why it matters.","I can practise round independently on a new prompt.","I can notice budget needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Money unit review connects totalling, comparing, change, rounding and budget decisions. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A guess with no working.","Money unit review connects totalling, comparing, change, rounding and budget decisions.","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about review?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nReview path: total coins → compare two amounts → find change → round to nearest dollar → decide if a cart fits a budget.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMini review: total; change from $10 for $7.35; round $7.35; enough for another $2.50 item from leftover?\n\nFEEDBACK\nChange $2.65; rounds to $7; leftover after $7.35 from $10 is $2.65 so $2.50 fits.","explain":"Money unit review connects totalling, comparing, change, rounding and budget decisions.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Review that only lists dollars without cents hides place errors. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Money unit skills linked in one path.","caption":"Keep cents visible throughout.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Review path</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">total → compare → change</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Then</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">round → budget</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Review path: total coins → compare two amounts → find change → round to nearest dollar → decide if a cart fits a budget. Then write your own working.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.\nJuno: Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Review path: total coins → compare two amounts → find change → round to nearest dollar → decide if a cart fits a budget. Then write your own working."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A calculation unrelated to the numbers given.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no working.","Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about review?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nReview path: total coins → compare two amounts → find change → round to nearest dollar → decide if a cart fits a budget. Look at each number, place or distance that carries the method.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite the key numbers from the model and say what each one represents in the method.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful working names the quantities and the reason for each step, not only the final answer.","explain":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only the final answer without working does not show the method. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Show the working","visual":[{"alt":"Review item linking change and a next buy.","caption":"List next practice goals.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Mini</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">change from $10 for $7.35</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Leftover</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">enough for $2.50?</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific mathematical error, then show the corrected working. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A guess with no working.","Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific mathematical error, then show the corrected working.","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about review?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nReview that only lists dollars without cents hides place errors. Here is a more productive model: Review path: total coins → compare two amounts → find change → round to nearest dollar → decide if a cart fits a budget.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach gives the wrong value or meaning, then write corrected working.\n\nFEEDBACK\nChange $2.65; rounds to $7; leftover after $7.35 from $10 is $2.65 so $2.50 fits.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific mathematical error, then show the corrected working.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the answer without repairing the method does not fix the error. Review that only lists dollars without cents hides place errors. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy and units or places. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A guess with no working.","Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy and units or places.","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about review?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nMoney unit review connects totalling, comparing, change, rounding and budget decisions. Now choose different numbers to show you can transfer the method.\n\nTRY FIRST\nSolve one fresh problem that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your answer.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new problem should use the same method as the model, but contain different numbers.","explain":"Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of 